    "q-45061": {
      "dom_id": "step-q45061",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+5,\\,\\,f(1)=4,\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=4,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(1)+5 \\\\  & =4+5 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =f(2)-f(1) \\\\  & =9-4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $f(1)=4$ and $d=5$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =4+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=5n-1,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
